==================================================================
DAY 23 - PART B: LAB
Create, use, decode, and protect Secrets
Time: 75-90 minutes
==================================================================

cd ~/k8s-journey/manifests/week4


STEP 1: Create Secrets
----------------------
k create secret generic db-cred --from-literal=username=app --from-literal=password='S3cr3t!'
k get secret db-cred
k describe secret db-cred        # sizes only, values hidden
k get secret db-cred -o yaml     # base64 values visible!


STEP 2: Decode it (prove base64 is not security)
------------------------------------------------
k get secret db-cred -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d ; echo

Anyone with "get secrets" permission can do this.


STEP 3: Secret in YAML with stringData
--------------------------------------
vi api-secret.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: api-key
type: Opaque
stringData:
  API_KEY: "abc123-not-real"
  config.json: |
    {"endpoint": "https://api.example.com", "token": "xyz"}

k apply -f api-secret.yaml
k get secret api-key -o yaml      # stored as base64 under data:

Add "api-secret.yaml" to .gitignore:
  echo "manifests/week4/api-secret.yaml" >> ~/k8s-journey/.gitignore


STEP 4: Use it as env and as files
----------------------------------
vi pod-secret.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: secret-demo
spec:
  containers:
  - name: c
    image: busybox:1.36
    command: ["sleep", "3600"]
    env:
    - name: DB_USER
      valueFrom:
        secretKeyRef:
          name: db-cred
          key: username
    - name: DB_PASS
      valueFrom:
        secretKeyRef:
          name: db-cred
          key: password
    volumeMounts:
    - name: api
      mountPath: /etc/secret
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: api
    secret:
      secretName: api-key
      defaultMode: 0400

k apply -f pod-secret.yaml
k exec secret-demo -- env | grep DB_
k exec secret-demo -- ls -l /etc/secret/
k exec secret-demo -- cat /etc/secret/config.json
k exec secret-demo -- mount | grep /etc/secret       # tmpfs


STEP 5: Where it lives on the node
----------------------------------
NODE=$(k get pod secret-demo -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}')
PUID=$(k get pod secret-demo -o jsonpath='{.metadata.uid}')
docker exec $NODE ls /var/lib/kubelet/pods/$PUID/volumes/kubernetes.io~secret/api
docker exec $NODE mount | grep $PUID | grep secret      # tmpfs = RAM


STEP 6: Where it lives in etcd (unencrypted!)
---------------------------------------------
docker exec lab-control-plane sh -c 'ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
  get /registry/secrets/default/db-cred' | strings | grep -i s3cr

If etcdctl is not in the node image, skip this step; Week 6 installs
it. The point: without encryption at rest, the password is readable
on the etcd disk.


STEP 7: TLS secret
------------------
openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 30 -newkey rsa:2048 -keyout k.key -out k.crt -subj "/CN=demo.local"
k create secret tls demo-tls --cert=k.crt --key=k.key
k get secret demo-tls -o yaml | grep -E "type|tls"
rm k.key k.crt


STEP 8: Private registry pull
-----------------------------
On hub.docker.com make a PRIVATE repo, e.g. YOURUSER/private-app, and
push an image to it:
  docker tag YOURUSER/myapp:v1 YOURUSER/private-app:v1
  docker push YOURUSER/private-app:v1

k run priv --image=YOURUSER/private-app:v1
k get pod priv                      # ErrImagePull (unauthorized)

Create an access token in Docker Hub (Account settings -> Personal
access tokens, read-only), then:
  k create secret docker-registry regcred \
    --docker-server=https://index.docker.io/v1/ \
    --docker-username=YOURUSER --docker-password=<TOKEN>
  k delete pod priv
  k run priv --image=YOURUSER/private-app:v1 \
    --overrides='{"spec":{"imagePullSecrets":[{"name":"regcred"}]}}'
  k get pod priv                    # Running

Or attach to the default ServiceAccount (all pods in the namespace):
  k patch sa default -p '{"imagePullSecrets":[{"name":"regcred"}]}'


STEP 9 (READ ONLY): Sealed Secrets idea
---------------------------------------
With the Sealed Secrets controller installed:
  k create secret generic x --from-literal=p=1 --dry-run=client -o yaml \
    | kubeseal -o yaml > sealed-x.yaml
sealed-x.yaml is encrypted and safe to commit. Only the controller in
the cluster can decrypt it. You'll see External Secrets in Week 7.


STEP 10: Clean up and commit
----------------------------
k delete pod secret-demo priv
k delete secret db-cred api-key demo-tls
cd ~/k8s-journey && git add . && git commit -m "day23: secrets"
(Check "git status" first: no keys or tokens should be committed.)
